1. Implement the `PhoneNumber` constructor to validate and clean up phone numbers.
2. Implement `getNumber()` to return the cleaned 10-digit number.
3. Throw `IllegalArgumentException` for invalid inputs (wrong length, invalid area/exchange codes, letters, invalid punctuation, etc.).

PhoneNumber.java
```
class PhoneNumber {

    private final String number;

    PhoneNumber(String numberString) {
        String digitsOnly = numberString.replaceAll("\\D", "");

        if (numberString.chars().anyMatch(Character::isLetter)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("letters not permitted");
        }

        if (digitsOnly.length() < 10) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("incorrect number of digits");
        }

        if (digitsOnly.length() > 11) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("more than 11 digits");
        }

        if (digitsOnly.length() == 11) {
            if (digitsOnly.charAt(0) != '1') {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("11 digits must start with 1");
            }
            digitsOnly = digitsOnly.substring(1);
        }

        if (digitsOnly.length() != 10) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("exactly 10 digits expected");
        }

        char areaCodeFirstDigit = digitsOnly.charAt(0);
        char exchangeCodeFirstDigit = digitsOnly.charAt(3);

        if (areaCodeFirstDigit == '0') {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("area code cannot start with 0");
        }

        if (areaCodeFirstDigit == '1') {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("area code cannot start with 1");
        }

        if (exchangeCodeFirstDigit == '0') {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("exchange code cannot start with 0");
        }

        if (exchangeCodeFirstDigit == '1') {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("exchange code cannot start with 1");
        }

        this.number = digitsOnly;
    }

    String getNumber() {
        return number;
    }
}
```
