Ok, I'll implement the bowling game scoring logic.

Let me think about the approach:
- Track all rolls in a list
- On `score()`, iterate through frames 1-10 and compute scores
- Handle strikes, spares, and fill balls for the 10th frame

BowlingGame.java
```java
class BowlingGame {
    private int[] rolls = new int[20];
    private int rollIndex = 0;

    void roll(int pins) {
        rolls[rollIndex++] = pins;
    }

    int score() {
        int score = 0;
        int index = 0;

        for (int frame = 0; frame < 10; frame++) {
            if (rolls[index] == 10) {
                // Strike
                score += 10 + rolls[index + 1] + rolls[index + 2];
                index++;
            } else if (rolls[index] + rolls[index + 1] == 10) {
                // Spare
                score += 10 + rolls[index + 2];
                index += 2;
            } else {
                // Open frame
                score += rolls[index] + rolls[index + 1];
                index += 2;
            }
        }

        return score;
    }
}
```
