I'll analyze the Queen Attack exercise and implement the solution.

The `QueenAttack` class needs to:
1. Accept white and black queen positions (or defaults)
2. Validate positions are on the board and not overlapping
3. Render the board with queens
4. Determine if queens can attack each other

queen-attack.js
```javascript
export class QueenAttack {
  constructor({
    black: [blackRow, blackColumn] = [7, 3],
    white: [whiteRow, whiteColumn] = [0, 3],
  } = {}) {
    if (
      (blackRow === whiteRow && blackColumn === whiteColumn) ||
      (whiteRow === blackRow && whiteColumn === blackColumn)
    ) {
      throw new Error('Queens cannot occupy the same position');
    }

    this.white = { row: whiteRow, column: whiteColumn };
    this.black = { row: blackRow, column: blackColumn };
  }

  toString() {
    const board = Array.from({ length: 8 }, () => Array({ length: 8 }).fill(' '));

    board[this.white.row][this.white.column] = 'W';
    board[this.black.row][this.black.column] = 'B';

    return board.map((row) => row.join(' ')).join('\n');
  }

  get canAttack() {
    const { row: whiteRow, column: whiteColumn } = this.white;
    const { row: blackRow, column: blackColumn } = this.black;

    const sameRow = whiteRow === blackRow;
    const sameColumn = whiteColumn === blackColumn;
    const sameDiagonal =
      Math.abs(whiteRow - blackRow) === Math.abs(whiteColumn - blackColumn);

    return sameRow || sameColumn || sameDiagonal;
  }
}
```
