The issue is that the `row` variable is out of scope in the inner loop, and the logic for adding newlines is incorrect. I'll rewrite the transpose method with a cleaner approach that correctly handles the padding rules.

Transpose.java
```java
public class Transpose {
    public String transpose(String toTranspose) {
        if (toTranspose == null) {
            return null;
        }

        String[] lines = toTranspose.split("\n", -1);
        int rowCount = lines.length;

        // Find the maximum length of any line
        int maxLen = 0;
        for (String line : lines) {
            if (line.length() > maxLen) {
                maxLen = line.length();
            }
        }

        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();

        for (int col = 0; col < maxLen; col++) {
            StringBuilder newRow = new StringBuilder();
            for (int row = 0; row < rowCount; row++) {
                String line = lines[row];
                if (col < line.length()) {
                    newRow.append(line.charAt(col));
                } else {
                    // Check if any subsequent row has a character at this column
                    boolean hasCharAfter = false;
                    for (int laterRow = row + 1; laterRow < rowCount; laterRow++) {
                        if (col < lines[laterRow].length()) {
                            hasCharAfter = true;
                            break;
                        }
                    }
                    // Pad with space only if there are characters after in later rows
                    if (hasCharAfter) {
                        newRow.append(' ');
                    }
                }
            }
            if (result.length() > 0) {
                result.append('\n');
            }
            result.append(newRow);
        }

        return result.toString();
    }
}
```
