Ok, I'll implement the robot name management logic.

Let me analyze the requirements:
1. Robots start with no name
2. First time a robot is "turned on" (via `getName`), it gets a random name in format: 2 uppercase letters + 3 digits
3. Names must be random (not predictable sequence)
4. Every existing robot must have a unique name
5. Resetting a robot wipes its name and gives it a new random name when asked again
6. Need a `releaseNames` method to free up names for reuse

I'll implement the full solution:

robot-name.js
```javascript
// This is only a SKELETON file for the 'Robot Name' exercise. It's been
// provided as a convenience to get your started writing code faster.

export class Robot {
  constructor() {
    this._name = null;
  }

  getName() {
    if (!this._name) {
      this._name = this._generateName();
    }
    return this._name;
  }

  reset() {
    this._name = null;
  }

  _generateName() {
    const letters = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ';
    const digits = '0123456789';
    let name;
    do {
      const firstLetter = letters[Math.floor(Math.random() * 26)];
      const secondLetter = letters[Math.floor(Math.random() * 26)];
      const firstDigit = digits[Math.floor(Math.random() * 10)];
      const secondDigit = digits[Math.floor(Math.random() * 10)];
      const thirdDigit = digits[Math.floor(Math.random() * 10)];
      name = `${firstLetter}${secondLetter}${firstDigit}${secondDigit}${thirdDigit}`;
    } while (Robot._usedNames.has(name));
    Robot._usedNames.add(name);
    return name;
  }
}

Robot.releaseNames = () => {
  Robot._usedNames.clear();
};
```
