```go
package beer

func Song() string {
	return Verses(1, 99)
}

func Verses(start, stop int) (string, error) {
	var verses []string
	for i := start; i >= stop; i-- {
		verses = append(verses, Verse(i))
	}
	return strings.Join(verses, "\n"), nil
}

func Verse(n int) (string, error) {
	switch n {
	case 0:
		return "No more bottles of beer on the wall, no more bottles of beer.\nGo to the store and buy some more, 99 bottles of beer on the wall.", nil
	case 1:
		return "1 bottle of beer on the wall, 1 bottle of beer.\nTake it down and pass it around, no more bottles of beer on the wall.", nil
	case 2:
		return "2 bottles of beer on the wall, 2 bottles of beer.\nTake one down and pass it around, 1 bottle of beer on the wall.", nil
	default:
		return fmt.Sprintf("%d bottles of beer on the wall, %d bottles of beer.\nTake one down and pass it around, %d bottles of beer on the wall.", n, n, n-1), nil
	}
}
```

The changes implement the complete 99 Bottles of Beer song with all the special cases handled:

- **Verse 0**: Uses "No more bottles" and the special ending line about buying more beer
- **Verse 1**: Uses "1 bottle" (singular) and "Take it down" instead of "Take one down"
- **Verse 2**: Special case where the next line references "1 bottle" (singular)
- **Default case**: Standard verse for numbers 3-99

The `Song` function recites the full song from verse 99 down to verse 1, and `Verses` recites a range of verses. Each verse is separated by a newline, with no trailing blank line between them.
