The tests are failing because my validation incorrectly rejects valid parent-child relationships. The condition `parentId < id` is actually valid — a parent can have a lower ID than its child. Let me fix the validation logic.

The correct rules:
- A record's parent ID must be strictly less than its own ID (except root, where parent == id == 0)
- If parentId >= id (and not root), it's invalid

Let me fix this:

BuildTree.java
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;

class BuildTree {

    TreeNode buildTree(ArrayList<Record> records) throws InvalidRecordsException {
        if (records.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }

        int size = records.size();

        // Validate that record IDs are exactly 0..size-1
        for (Record record : records) {
            int id = record.getRecordId();
            if (id < 0 || id >= size) {
                throw new InvalidRecordsException("Invalid Records");
            }
        }

        // Build a map from record ID to TreeNode
        HashMap<Integer, TreeNode> nodeById = new HashMap<>();
        for (Record record : records) {
            nodeById.put(record.getRecordId(), new TreeNode(record.getRecordId()));
        }

        // Validate and link parent-child relationships
        for (Record record : records) {
            int id = record.getRecordId();
            int parentId = record.getParentId();

            if (id == parentId) {
                // Root record
                if (id != 0) {
                    throw new InvalidRecordsException("Invalid Records");
                }
                continue;
            }

            if (parentId < id) {
                throw new InvalidRecordsException("Invalid Records");
            }

            if (!nodeById.containsKey(parentId)) {
                throw new InvalidRecordsException("Invalid Records");
            }

            nodeById.get(parentId).getChildren().add(nodeById.get(id));
        }

        return nodeById.get(0);
    }

}
